      "chapter_slug": null,
      "movie_page_slug": null,
      "notes_md": "From 2026: I remember being in two minds about Gravity when compiling the shortlist for the book. I enjoyed it - I'm a Cuarón fan - but it is quite one-note and lacks nuance at a level where it opens up multiple conversations. There also wasn't much room in the book for a near Earth orbit film like this. That said, with low earth orbit becoming increasingly crowded, it's probably a film to come back to!\n\nClaude would like to add: Gravity has aged into a film that is no longer only about one astronaut's survival after an orbital accident — it is now, more urgently, the clearest cinematic articulation of what a Kessler-syndrome cascade looks like at human scale. The film's inciting incident is a destructive Russian anti-satellite weapons test whose debris field sets off a chain of collisions that sweeps through the altitude bands other spacecraft occupy. In 2013 this was a premise. In 2025 it is a policy conversation in the ITU and the FCC, with more than eleven thousand active satellites in orbit and Starlink alone nearing ten thousand. The film's technical consultants got it substantially right; it is the film's willingness to sit with what happens after the cascade starts that makes it indispensable alongside the new [orbital infrastructure page](https://spoileralert.wtf/md-files/p18_orbital_infrastructure.md). Also: Cuarón's long-take sequences do something for the felt experience of the orbital environment that policy documents never will. If the Kessler conversation is going to stay coherent for a general audience, it will be partly because this film exists. [Andrew - I have to agree with this!]\n\n---",
      "andrew_response_md": "",
      "summary": "A medical engineer on her first space mission and a veteran astronaut are stranded in orbit after debris destroys their shuttle. Survival is a sequence of dwindling oxygen and dwindling options."
