      "movie_page_slug": null,
      "notes_md": "From 2026: I didn't watch Akira until after I'd written Films from the Future, which explains its absence on the shortlist of movies. It was actually introduced to me by students in my Moviegoer's Guide to the Future class, and we watched it one year as the bonus movie. It is, it has to be said, a complex anime movie that requires a lot of attention to follow and make sense of. It's also highly polarizing in terms of fans who love it, and viewers who find it tedious and inpenetrable. But a lot to chew over in the movie when thinking about tech, society, and the future - and a classic sci fi anime movie to boot.\n\nFrom Claude: *Akira* is the one the cyberpunk tradition measures itself against. Otomo's 1988 anime — adapted from his own manga — takes place in Neo-Tokyo thirty-one years after the city was destroyed in a catastrophic blast. The government has been conducting secret experiments on children with latent psychic ability. One of them, Tetsuo, acquires powers he cannot control, and the film becomes a meditation on what happens when biotechnology and power are placed in hands that were never meant to hold them — and on who gets to decide which children are expendable. The thematic payload runs directly into *Ghost in the Shell*'s territory (which is in the book) and into the *Never Let Me Go* wrong-question frame (instrumentalised subjects whose humanity is quietly negotiated away). It also happens to be one of the most formally ambitious films in animation history, with a visual lexicon that defined a generation of what sci-fi could look like. The overlap with the book's frameworks is not subtle; the omission is easier to explain as scope than as oversight.\n\n---",
      "andrew_response_md": "",
      "summary": "In a post-apocalyptic Neo-Tokyo, a teenage biker gang member develops uncontrollable psychic powers after a road accident, drawing the attention of a secret government program."
