      "notes_md": "From 2026: I can't believe I missed the Back to the Future Films in my initial shortlist for the book! A bit of a blind spit, but it may be that when i last watched the original it didn't resonate as much as it did first time round. That said, there are themes across the three films that probably resonate more now than they did back in 2018 — drfinitely worth a revisit with a futures-critical eye!\n\nAnd, Claude decided to also weigh in here: Claude: It is also a film that almost uniquely rewards repeat viewing from a book-of-ideas angle. The causal structure is so tight that every scene becomes a small lesson in how systems respond to perturbation. And the film itself has become part of the cultural conversation about technology in ways that make it more, not less, thematically relevant — \"we have the hoverboards, just not the way we were promised\" is now a standard rhetorical move in hype-vs-reality conversations.\n\n---",
      "andrew_response_md": "",
      "summary": "A teenager is propelled back to 1955 in a DeLorean built by an eccentric inventor friend, where he must arrange the meeting of his own parents to ensure he still exists. Two sequels stretch the time-travel game across centuries."
