      "movie_page_slug": null,
      "notes_md": "From 2026: Carpenter's *The Thing* is a film that I really like, and looking back I'm not sure why it didn't make the initial shortlist — although this may be because I was struggling to find enough nuance in it. Well worth exploring further through the themes of the book though — and emerging conversations around advanced technologies and the future, and how we handle them. \n\nAnd a word from Claude: Worth pairing with Carpenter's technical restraint — practical effects, long silences, a cold that you can feel from the screen — against the more polished recent pandemic films like *Contagion*. The paranoia is doing something different from the information cascade in *Contagion*; it is showing what happens when the epistemic tools themselves fail. That is a framework the book's \"what we know and how we know it\" threads (through *Contact*) were designed for.\n\n---",
      "andrew_response_md": "",
      "summary": "An Antarctic research team uncovers a parasitic alien organism that perfectly imitates the people it kills. Trust collapses as no one can be sure who is still themselves. John Carpenter's 1982 version."
